{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjC6R8VkrEuoLjkDsLWG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D4FgYrBoqtCYYi87RsmDtU","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/lib/kit/README.md --jq '.content' | base64 -d | sed -n '1,120p'; echo ......; gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/lib/kit/post.js --jq '.content' | base64 -d; echo ......; gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/lib/kit/renderer.js --jq '.content' | base64 -d","description":"Skim the hub kit's readme and renderer helpers"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7159,"cache_read_input_tokens":82792,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7159},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9db0da04-284f-40b5-8e80-2e3f7ed2ab1c","uuid":"cc65c667-5f5b-46b2-a685-e5c25cdab247","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:00:23.819Z","request_id":"req_011CgjC6Qu6v8JdW17bCTwWf","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01D4FgYrBoqtCYYi87RsmDtU":{"command":"gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/lib/kit/README.md --jq '.content' | base64 -d | sed -n '1,120p'; echo ......; gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/lib/kit/post.js --jq '.content' | base64 -d; echo ......; gh api repos/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/contents/lib/kit/renderer.js --jq '.content' | base64 -d","description":"Skim the hub kit's readme and renderer helpers"}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01D4FgYrBoqtCYYi87RsmDtU","type":"tool_result","content":"# The 3D kit\n\nGames using the shared kit run on one foundation. Imported games that retain\ntheir own engine version use the separate shelf in `../vendor/README.md`.\n\n| Piece | Version | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| three.js | r186 (0.186.1), `WebGPURenderer` from `three/webgpu`, WebGL2 fallback | `/lib/three/` |\n| Rapier | 0.20.0 (SIMD build where wasm SIMD exists, plain build otherwise) | `/lib/rapier/` |\n| The kit | boot, renderer, quality tiers, settings menu, input, touch, physics, post, points | `/lib/kit/` |\n\nThe rule of thumb: **share the foundation, not the gameplay.** Hosting, startup,\nrenderer setup, quality tiers, the settings panel, input plumbing and physics\nsetup come from here. Every game keeps its own mechanics, feel and art. Games\nstay comparable because they follow the same conventions (versions, units,\naction names), not because they share gameplay code.\n\n`example.html` in this folder is a complete small game that uses every piece.\nRead it before migrating anything.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The page head\n\nEvery 3D game carries this block, unchanged, in `<head>` before any module\nscript. No game embeds or downloads its own copy of three.js, and nothing\nloads from a CDN.\n\n```html\n<script type=\"importmap\">\n{\n  \"imports\": {\n    \"three\": \"/lib/three/three.webgpu.js\",\n    \"three/webgpu\": \"/lib/three/three.webgpu.js\",\n    \"three/tsl\": \"/lib/three/three.tsl.js\",\n    \"three/addons/\": \"/lib/three/addons/\",\n    \"kit/\": \"/lib/kit/\"\n  }\n}\n</script>\n<link rel=\"modulepreload\" href=\"/lib/three/three.core.js\" />\n<link rel=\"modulepreload\" href=\"/lib/three/three.webgpu.js\" />\n```\n\n- `three` and `three/webgpu` are the same file, so addons that import\n  `'three'` share one copy. `WebGLRenderer` does not exist in it, on purpose.\n- Addons live under `three/addons/` exactly as in `examples/jsm/`. WebGL-only\n  ones (EffectComposer and `postprocessing/`, `shaders/`, `Sky.js`,\n  `Water.js`, `Reflector.js`, `Lensflare.js`, `CSM.js`) are not shipped. Use\n  their node versions: `objects/SkyMesh.js`, `objects/WaterMesh.js`,\n  `objects/Water2Mesh.js`, TSL `reflector()`, `objects/LensflareMesh.js`,\n  `csm/CSMShadowNode.js`, `lines/webgpu/Line2.js`.\n- Keep the hub's head tags: `<title>`, `<meta name=\"description\">`,\n  `<meta name=\"theme-color\">`, the icon link and `og:image`. The wall reads them.\n\n## 2. Boot\n\n```js\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { boot } from 'kit/boot.js';\n\nconst kit = await boot({\n  title: 'Skybreak',        // loading card; defaults to document.title\n  physics: true,            // loads Rapier → kit.RAPIER (omit if the game has no physics engine)\n  input: { /* actions, see §4 */ },\n  // renderer: { antialias: false },  // e.g. a deliberately pixelated look\n  // mount: document.querySelector('#stage'),  // if the canvas lives in a panel, not the window\n  // canvas: existingCanvas,  // reuse a canvas the page already has\n  // gear: 'tl',              // settings button corner: tr (default), tl, br, bl, none\n  // holdLoading: true,       // keep the loading card until kit.loading.done()\n});\n\nconst scene = new THREE.Scene();\nconst camera = kit.fitCamera(new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(60, 1, 0.1, 500));\nawait kit.renderer.compileAsync(scene, camera);   // optional: no shader hitches on the first frames\n\nkit.start((dt, t) => {           // dt in seconds (clamped to 0.1), t in seconds\n  update(dt);\n  kit.renderer.render(scene, camera);   // or post.render()\n});\n```\n\nWhat `boot()` does, so games don't:\n\n- Shows a loading card, then removes it after the first frame (or on\n  `kit.loading.done()` with `holdLoading`). Use `kit.loading.progress(0.5, 'Loading textures…')`\n  while building. A game with its own title screen keeps it: the card only\n  covers startup.\n- Starts `WebGPURenderer`: WebGPU with a conservative device (see §6), WebGL2\n  if WebGPU is missing, refuses, or failed on this device before. If the GPU\n  device is lost it says so and offers a restart on WebGL2.\n- Picks the quality tier, sets the pixel ratio, sizes the canvas to the window\n  (or `mount`) and keeps it sized. Dynamic resolution holds the frame rate\n  when the Resolution setting is Auto.\n- Wires keyboard, mouse, gamepad and touch into `kit.input` and builds the\n  on-screen touch controls from the actions.\n- Adds the settings gear (also **F9**, and `kit.menu.open()` for a game's own\n  pause menu). Opening it pauses the loop.\n- Runs the loop with `setAnimationLoop`. Never call `requestAnimationFrame` for\n  the game loop yourself.\n- Publishes `window.__kit` (`backend`, `tier`, `frames`, `fps`, `errors` with\n  a `count` on repeats, `ready`, `bootMs`, `pipelines`, `pipelineLog`) for\n  automated checks.\n\nThe kit object:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `kit.renderer` | the initialised `WebGPURenderer` |\n| `kit.backend` | `'webgpu'` or `'webgl2'`; `kit.backendLabel` for a HUD |\n| `kit.tier` | `{ name, pixelRatio, msaa, shadowMapSize, post, detail, drawDistance }` |\n| `kit.onQuality(fn)` | the player changed quality: re-apply shadow sizes, counts, draw distance |\n| `kit.shadow(light, 2048)` | size a light's shadow map for the tier (2048 at *high*) and keep it sized when quality changes; `kit.shadowSize(2048)` gives just the number |\n| `kit.detail(count)` | a count (particles, props, crowd) scaled for the tier |\n| `kit.input`, `kit.touch` | §4 |\n| `kit.fitCamera(cam)`, `kit.onResize(fn)`, `kit.size` | sizing |\n| `kit.start(fn)`, `kit.stop()`, `kit.pause(bool)`, `kit.onPause(fn)` | the loop (pause audio in `onPause`) |\n| `kit.menu` | `open()`, `close()`, `toggle()`, `isOpen`, `setGear(corner)` |\n| `kit.RAPIER` | Rapier namespace when `physics: true` |\n| `kit.setPixelHeight(lines)` | fixed low-resolution rendering with hard pixels (0 = native), §5 |\n......\n/**\n * post.js — the standard post-processing chain, in TSL so it runs the same\n * on WebGPU and WebGL2. It replaces EffectComposer, which WebGPURenderer\n * cannot use.\n *\n *   const post = createPost(kit, scene, camera, { bloom: { strength: 0.8, radius: 0.4, threshold: 0.8 } });\n *   kit.start((dt) => { update(dt); post.render(); });\n *\n * Anti-aliasing follows the tier: MSAA on high and ultra (set on the\n * renderer, inherited by the scene pass), FXAA on medium, none on low.\n * Bloom stays on at every tier and only renders smaller on 'lite'.\n *\n * For anything else, pass `output(color, ctx)` and build on the scene\n * colour with TSL: vignette, colour grading, film grain, outlines…\n */\n\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { pass, renderOutput } from 'three/tsl';\nimport { bloom as bloomNode } from 'three/addons/tsl/display/BloomNode.js';\nimport { fxaa as fxaaNode } from 'three/addons/tsl/display/FXAANode.js';\n\n/**\n * @param {object} kit       from boot()\n * @param {THREE.Scene} scene\n * @param {THREE.Camera} camera\n * @param {object} [opts]\n * @param {{strength?:number, radius?:number, threshold?:number}|false} [opts.bloom]\n * @param {boolean|'auto'} [opts.fxaa='auto']  'auto' = FXAA when the tier has no MSAA (except on low)\n * @param {(color: any, ctx: object) => any} [opts.output]  extra TSL on the linear scene colour\n */\nexport function createPost(kit, scene, camera, { bloom = false, fxaa = 'auto', output } = {}) {\n  const pipeline = new THREE.RenderPipeline(kit.renderer);\n  const scenePass = pass(scene, camera);\n  const color = scenePass.getTextureNode('output');\n\n  let glow = null;\n  if (bloom) glow = bloomNode(color, bloom.strength ?? 1, bloom.radius ?? 0.4, bloom.threshold ?? 0.85);\n\n  // Rebuilt when the player changes quality, so FXAA and the bloom size follow the tier.\n  const build = (tier) => {\n    let node = color;\n    if (glow) {\n      glow.setResolutionScale(tier.post === 'lite' ? 0.35 : 0.5);\n      node = node.add(glow);\n    }\n    if (output) node = output(node, { scenePass, color, pipeline, tier }) ?? node;\n    const useFxaa = fxaa === true || (fxaa === 'auto' && !tier.msaa && tier.name !== 'low');\n    // FXAA works on display-referred colour, so tone mapping and sRGB go first.\n    pipeline.outputColorTransform = !useFxaa;\n    pipeline.outputNode = useFxaa ? fxaaNode(renderOutput(node)) : node;\n    pipeline.needsUpdate = true;\n  };\n  build(kit.tier);\n  const off = kit.onQuality?.(build);\n\n  return {\n    pipeline,\n    scenePass,\n    bloom: glow,\n    render() {\n      pipeline.render();\n    },\n    dispose() {\n      off?.();\n      pipeline.dispose();\n    },\n  };\n}\n......\n/**\n * renderer.js — one way to start three.js r186's WebGPURenderer everywhere.\n *\n * WebGPU first, WebGL2 when the browser has no WebGPU, when the adapter or\n * device refuses, or when WebGPU already failed on this device. Both backends\n * run the same node materials, so they draw the same scene.\n *\n * The WebGPU device is created here rather than by three.js so the default\n * can be conservative: three.js asks for every optional feature the adapter\n * lists, and a few of those (shader-f16, subgroups, timestamp queries,\n * dual-source blending) are where mobile drivers have misbehaved. The\n * \"Advanced WebGPU features\" setting hands device creation back to three.js,\n * which then requests everything.\n */\n\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { chooseBackend, deviceMemory, webgpuFailedRecently } from './settings.js';\n\n/** Optional features that are widely supported and safe to request. */\nexport const SAFE_FEATURES = [\n  'core-features-and-limits',\n  'depth-clip-control',\n  'depth32float-stencil8',\n  'float32-filterable',\n  'float32-blendable',\n  'rg11b10ufloat-renderable',\n  'indirect-first-instance',\n  'texture-compression-bc',\n  'texture-compression-etc2',\n  'texture-compression-astc',\n];\n\nasync function conservativeDevice() {\n  const adapter = await navigator.gpu.requestAdapter({ powerPreference: 'high-performance' });\n  if (!adapter) throw new Error('No WebGPU adapter');\n  const requiredFeatures = SAFE_FEATURES.filter((f) => adapter.features.has(f));\n  const device = await adapter.requestDevice({ requiredFeatures });\n  return { adapter, device };\n}\n\n/**\n * @param {object} opts\n * @param {HTMLCanvasElement} [opts.canvas]\n * @param {'auto'|'webgpu'|'webgl2'} [opts.backend]\n * @param {boolean} [opts.advanced]  let three.js request every adapter feature\n * @param {object} [opts.params]     extra WebGPURenderer parameters (antialias, alpha, …)\n * @param {(info: object) => void} [opts.onDeviceLost]\n * @returns {Promise<{ renderer: THREE.WebGPURenderer, backend: 'webgpu'|'webgl2', adapterInfo: object|null, fellBack: string|null }>}\n */\nexport async function createRenderer({ canvas, backend = 'auto', advanced = false, params = {}, onDeviceLost } = {}) {\n  // `canvas` may be replaced (see build); callers use renderer.domElement afterwards.\n  const memory = deviceMemory.read();\n  const wanted = chooseBackend(backend, {\n    hasWebGPU: typeof navigator !== 'undefined' && !!navigator.gpu,\n    webgpuFailed: webgpuFailedRecently(memory),\n  });\n\n  let fellBack = null;\n  let adapterInfo = null;\n\n  const build = async (useWebGPU) => {\n    const extra = {};\n    if (useWebGPU && !advanced) {\n      const { adapter, device } = await conservativeDevice();\n      extra.device = device;\n      adapterInfo = adapter.info ? { vendor: adapter.info.vendor, architecture: adapter.info.architecture } : null;\n    }\n    if (!useWebGPU && canvas) {\n      // three.js asks for an antialiased WebGL2 context only if it plans to render through its\n      // own framebuffer target at init. A game that switches to linear output or no tone mapping\n      // afterwards then draws straight into a canvas without MSAA, and WebGL2 loses the\n      // anti-aliasing WebGPU keeps. Creating the context here keeps the two backends alike.\n      const attrs = { antialias: !!(params.antialias || params.samples), alpha: true, depth: params.depth ?? true, stencil: params.stencil ?? false, powerPreference: 'high-performance' };\n      let gl = canvas.getContext('webgl2', attrs);\n      if (!gl) {\n        // The canvas already holds another context (a failed WebGPU start): swap in a fresh one.\n        const fresh = canvas.cloneNode(false);\n        canvas.replaceWith(fresh);\n        canvas = fresh;\n        gl = canvas.getContext('webgl2', attrs);\n      }\n      if (gl) extra.context = gl;\n    }\n    const renderer = new THREE.WebGPURenderer({\n      canvas,\n      powerPreference: 'high-performance',\n      ...params,\n      ...extra,\n      forceWebGL: !useWebGPU,\n    });\n    await renderer.init();\n    return renderer;\n  };\n\n  let renderer;\n  if (wanted === 'webgpu') {\n    try {\n      renderer = await build(true);\n    } catch (err) {\n      fellBack = String(err?.message || err);\n      console.warn('[kit] WebGPU start failed, using WebGL2:', fellBack);\n      renderer = await build(false);\n    }\n  } else {\n    renderer = await build(false);\n  }\n\n  const active = renderer.backend?.isWebGPUBackend ? 'webgpu' : 'webgl2';\n  if (wanted === 'webgpu' && active !== 'webgpu' && !fellBack) fellBack = 'three.js fell back to WebGL2';\n\n  const reportLost = renderer.onDeviceLost.bind(renderer);\n  renderer.onDeviceLost = (info) => {\n    reportLost(info);\n    if (active === 'webgpu') deviceMemory.write({ webgpuFailed: Date.now(), reason: String(info?.message || 'device lost') });\n    onDeviceLost?.(info, active);\n  };\n\n  return { renderer, backend: active, adapterInfo, fellBack };\n}\n\n/** A human label for HUDs and the settings panel. */\nexport const backendLabel = (backend) => (backend === 'webgpu' ? 'WebGPU' : 'WebGL2');","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9db0da04-284f-40b5-8e80-2e3f7ed2ab1c","uuid":"0fe2bdb0-8547-47a5-85ef-fa8254b7462f","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:00:25.102Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"# The 3D kit\n\nGames using the shared kit run on one foundation. Imported games that retain\ntheir own engine version use the separate shelf in `../vendor/README.md`.\n\n| Piece | Version | Where |\n|---|---|---|\n| three.js | r186 (0.186.1), `WebGPURenderer` from `three/webgpu`, WebGL2 fallback | `/lib/three/` |\n| Rapier | 0.20.0 (SIMD build where wasm SIMD exists, plain build otherwise) | `/lib/rapier/` |\n| The kit | boot, renderer, quality tiers, settings menu, input, touch, physics, post, points | `/lib/kit/` |\n\nThe rule of thumb: **share the foundation, not the gameplay.** Hosting, startup,\nrenderer setup, quality tiers, the settings panel, input plumbing and physics\nsetup come from here. Every game keeps its own mechanics, feel and art. Games\nstay comparable because they follow the same conventions (versions, units,\naction names), not because they share gameplay code.\n\n`example.html` in this folder is a complete small game that uses every piece.\nRead it before migrating anything.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The page head\n\nEvery 3D game carries this block, unchanged, in `<head>` before any module\nscript. No game embeds or downloads its own copy of three.js, and nothing\nloads from a CDN.\n\n```html\n<script type=\"importmap\">\n{\n  \"imports\": {\n    \"three\": \"/lib/three/three.webgpu.js\",\n    \"three/webgpu\": \"/lib/three/three.webgpu.js\",\n    \"three/tsl\": \"/lib/three/three.tsl.js\",\n    \"three/addons/\": \"/lib/three/addons/\",\n    \"kit/\": \"/lib/kit/\"\n  }\n}\n</script>\n<link rel=\"modulepreload\" href=\"/lib/three/three.core.js\" />\n<link rel=\"modulepreload\" href=\"/lib/three/three.webgpu.js\" />\n```\n\n- `three` and `three/webgpu` are the same file, so addons that import\n  `'three'` share one copy. `WebGLRenderer` does not exist in it, on purpose.\n- Addons live under `three/addons/` exactly as in `examples/jsm/`. WebGL-only\n  ones (EffectComposer and `postprocessing/`, `shaders/`, `Sky.js`,\n  `Water.js`, `Reflector.js`, `Lensflare.js`, `CSM.js`) are not shipped. Use\n  their node versions: `objects/SkyMesh.js`, `objects/WaterMesh.js`,\n  `objects/Water2Mesh.js`, TSL `reflector()`, `objects/LensflareMesh.js`,\n  `csm/CSMShadowNode.js`, `lines/webgpu/Line2.js`.\n- Keep the hub's head tags: `<title>`, `<meta name=\"description\">`,\n  `<meta name=\"theme-color\">`, the icon link and `og:image`. The wall reads them.\n\n## 2. Boot\n\n```js\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { boot } from 'kit/boot.js';\n\nconst kit = await boot({\n  title: 'Skybreak',        // loading card; defaults to document.title\n  physics: true,            // loads Rapier → kit.RAPIER (omit if the game has no physics engine)\n  input: { /* actions, see §4 */ },\n  // renderer: { antialias: false },  // e.g. a deliberately pixelated look\n  // mount: document.querySelector('#stage'),  // if the canvas lives in a panel, not the window\n  // canvas: existingCanvas,  // reuse a canvas the page already has\n  // gear: 'tl',              // settings button corner: tr (default), tl, br, bl, none\n  // holdLoading: true,       // keep the loading card until kit.loading.done()\n});\n\nconst scene = new THREE.Scene();\nconst camera = kit.fitCamera(new THREE.PerspectiveCamera(60, 1, 0.1, 500));\nawait kit.renderer.compileAsync(scene, camera);   // optional: no shader hitches on the first frames\n\nkit.start((dt, t) => {           // dt in seconds (clamped to 0.1), t in seconds\n  update(dt);\n  kit.renderer.render(scene, camera);   // or post.render()\n});\n```\n\nWhat `boot()` does, so games don't:\n\n- Shows a loading card, then removes it after the first frame (or on\n  `kit.loading.done()` with `holdLoading`). Use `kit.loading.progress(0.5, 'Loading textures…')`\n  while building. A game with its own title screen keeps it: the card only\n  covers startup.\n- Starts `WebGPURenderer`: WebGPU with a conservative device (see §6), WebGL2\n  if WebGPU is missing, refuses, or failed on this device before. If the GPU\n  device is lost it says so and offers a restart on WebGL2.\n- Picks the quality tier, sets the pixel ratio, sizes the canvas to the window\n  (or `mount`) and keeps it sized. Dynamic resolution holds the frame rate\n  when the Resolution setting is Auto.\n- Wires keyboard, mouse, gamepad and touch into `kit.input` and builds the\n  on-screen touch controls from the actions.\n- Adds the settings gear (also **F9**, and `kit.menu.open()` for a game's own\n  pause menu). Opening it pauses the loop.\n- Runs the loop with `setAnimationLoop`. Never call `requestAnimationFrame` for\n  the game loop yourself.\n- Publishes `window.__kit` (`backend`, `tier`, `frames`, `fps`, `errors` with\n  a `count` on repeats, `ready`, `bootMs`, `pipelines`, `pipelineLog`) for\n  automated checks.\n\nThe kit object:\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| `kit.renderer` | the initialised `WebGPURenderer` |\n| `kit.backend` | `'webgpu'` or `'webgl2'`; `kit.backendLabel` for a HUD |\n| `kit.tier` | `{ name, pixelRatio, msaa, shadowMapSize, post, detail, drawDistance }` |\n| `kit.onQuality(fn)` | the player changed quality: re-apply shadow sizes, counts, draw distance |\n| `kit.shadow(light, 2048)` | size a light's shadow map for the tier (2048 at *high*) and keep it sized when quality changes; `kit.shadowSize(2048)` gives just the number |\n| `kit.detail(count)` | a count (particles, props, crowd) scaled for the tier |\n| `kit.input`, `kit.touch` | §4 |\n| `kit.fitCamera(cam)`, `kit.onResize(fn)`, `kit.size` | sizing |\n| `kit.start(fn)`, `kit.stop()`, `kit.pause(bool)`, `kit.onPause(fn)` | the loop (pause audio in `onPause`) |\n| `kit.menu` | `open()`, `close()`, `toggle()`, `isOpen`, `setGear(corner)` |\n| `kit.RAPIER` | Rapier namespace when `physics: true` |\n| `kit.setPixelHeight(lines)` | fixed low-resolution rendering with hard pixels (0 = native), §5 |\n......\n/**\n * post.js — the standard post-processing chain, in TSL so it runs the same\n * on WebGPU and WebGL2. It replaces EffectComposer, which WebGPURenderer\n * cannot use.\n *\n *   const post = createPost(kit, scene, camera, { bloom: { strength: 0.8, radius: 0.4, threshold: 0.8 } });\n *   kit.start((dt) => { update(dt); post.render(); });\n *\n * Anti-aliasing follows the tier: MSAA on high and ultra (set on the\n * renderer, inherited by the scene pass), FXAA on medium, none on low.\n * Bloom stays on at every tier and only renders smaller on 'lite'.\n *\n * For anything else, pass `output(color, ctx)` and build on the scene\n * colour with TSL: vignette, colour grading, film grain, outlines…\n */\n\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { pass, renderOutput } from 'three/tsl';\nimport { bloom as bloomNode } from 'three/addons/tsl/display/BloomNode.js';\nimport { fxaa as fxaaNode } from 'three/addons/tsl/display/FXAANode.js';\n\n/**\n * @param {object} kit       from boot()\n * @param {THREE.Scene} scene\n * @param {THREE.Camera} camera\n * @param {object} [opts]\n * @param {{strength?:number, radius?:number, threshold?:number}|false} [opts.bloom]\n * @param {boolean|'auto'} [opts.fxaa='auto']  'auto' = FXAA when the tier has no MSAA (except on low)\n * @param {(color: any, ctx: object) => any} [opts.output]  extra TSL on the linear scene colour\n */\nexport function createPost(kit, scene, camera, { bloom = false, fxaa = 'auto', output } = {}) {\n  const pipeline = new THREE.RenderPipeline(kit.renderer);\n  const scenePass = pass(scene, camera);\n  const color = scenePass.getTextureNode('output');\n\n  let glow = null;\n  if (bloom) glow = bloomNode(color, bloom.strength ?? 1, bloom.radius ?? 0.4, bloom.threshold ?? 0.85);\n\n  // Rebuilt when the player changes quality, so FXAA and the bloom size follow the tier.\n  const build = (tier) => {\n    let node = color;\n    if (glow) {\n      glow.setResolutionScale(tier.post === 'lite' ? 0.35 : 0.5);\n      node = node.add(glow);\n    }\n    if (output) node = output(node, { scenePass, color, pipeline, tier }) ?? node;\n    const useFxaa = fxaa === true || (fxaa === 'auto' && !tier.msaa && tier.name !== 'low');\n    // FXAA works on display-referred colour, so tone mapping and sRGB go first.\n    pipeline.outputColorTransform = !useFxaa;\n    pipeline.outputNode = useFxaa ? fxaaNode(renderOutput(node)) : node;\n    pipeline.needsUpdate = true;\n  };\n  build(kit.tier);\n  const off = kit.onQuality?.(build);\n\n  return {\n    pipeline,\n    scenePass,\n    bloom: glow,\n    render() {\n      pipeline.render();\n    },\n    dispose() {\n      off?.();\n      pipeline.dispose();\n    },\n  };\n}\n......\n/**\n * renderer.js — one way to start three.js r186's WebGPURenderer everywhere.\n *\n * WebGPU first, WebGL2 when the browser has no WebGPU, when the adapter or\n * device refuses, or when WebGPU already failed on this device. Both backends\n * run the same node materials, so they draw the same scene.\n *\n * The WebGPU device is created here rather than by three.js so the default\n * can be conservative: three.js asks for every optional feature the adapter\n * lists, and a few of those (shader-f16, subgroups, timestamp queries,\n * dual-source blending) are where mobile drivers have misbehaved. The\n * \"Advanced WebGPU features\" setting hands device creation back to three.js,\n * which then requests everything.\n */\n\nimport * as THREE from 'three/webgpu';\nimport { chooseBackend, deviceMemory, webgpuFailedRecently } from './settings.js';\n\n/** Optional features that are widely supported and safe to request. */\nexport const SAFE_FEATURES = [\n  'core-features-and-limits',\n  'depth-clip-control',\n  'depth32float-stencil8',\n  'float32-filterable',\n  'float32-blendable',\n  'rg11b10ufloat-renderable',\n  'indirect-first-instance',\n  'texture-compression-bc',\n  'texture-compression-etc2',\n  'texture-compression-astc',\n];\n\nasync function conservativeDevice() {\n  const adapter = await navigator.gpu.requestAdapter({ powerPreference: 'high-performance' });\n  if (!adapter) throw new Error('No WebGPU adapter');\n  const requiredFeatures = SAFE_FEATURES.filter((f) => adapter.features.has(f));\n  const device = await adapter.requestDevice({ requiredFeatures });\n  return { adapter, device };\n}\n\n/**\n * @param {object} opts\n * @param {HTMLCanvasElement} [opts.canvas]\n * @param {'auto'|'webgpu'|'webgl2'} [opts.backend]\n * @param {boolean} [opts.advanced]  let three.js request every adapter feature\n * @param {object} [opts.params]     extra WebGPURenderer parameters (antialias, alpha, …)\n * @param {(info: object) => void} [opts.onDeviceLost]\n * @returns {Promise<{ renderer: THREE.WebGPURenderer, backend: 'webgpu'|'webgl2', adapterInfo: object|null, fellBack: string|null }>}\n */\nexport async function createRenderer({ canvas, backend = 'auto', advanced = false, params = {}, onDeviceLost } = {}) {\n  // `canvas` may be replaced (see build); callers use renderer.domElement afterwards.\n  const memory = deviceMemory.read();\n  const wanted = chooseBackend(backend, {\n    hasWebGPU: typeof navigator !== 'undefined' && !!navigator.gpu,\n    webgpuFailed: webgpuFailedRecently(memory),\n  });\n\n  let fellBack = null;\n  let adapterInfo = null;\n\n  const build = async (useWebGPU) => {\n    const extra = {};\n    if (useWebGPU && !advanced) {\n      const { adapter, device } = await conservativeDevice();\n      extra.device = device;\n      adapterInfo = adapter.info ? { vendor: adapter.info.vendor, architecture: adapter.info.architecture } : null;\n    }\n    if (!useWebGPU && canvas) {\n      // three.js asks for an antialiased WebGL2 context only if it plans to render through its\n      // own framebuffer target at init. A game that switches to linear output or no tone mapping\n      // afterwards then draws straight into a canvas without MSAA, and WebGL2 loses the\n      // anti-aliasing WebGPU keeps. Creating the context here keeps the two backends alike.\n      const attrs = { antialias: !!(params.antialias || params.samples), alpha: true, depth: params.depth ?? true, stencil: params.stencil ?? false, powerPreference: 'high-performance' };\n      let gl = canvas.getContext('webgl2', attrs);\n      if (!gl) {\n        // The canvas already holds another context (a failed WebGPU start): swap in a fresh one.\n        const fresh = canvas.cloneNode(false);\n        canvas.replaceWith(fresh);\n        canvas = fresh;\n        gl = canvas.getContext('webgl2', attrs);\n      }\n      if (gl) extra.context = gl;\n    }\n    const renderer = new THREE.WebGPURenderer({\n      canvas,\n      powerPreference: 'high-performance',\n      ...params,\n      ...extra,\n      forceWebGL: !useWebGPU,\n    });\n    await renderer.init();\n    return renderer;\n  };\n\n  let renderer;\n  if (wanted === 'webgpu') {\n    try {\n      renderer = await build(true);\n    } catch (err) {\n      fellBack = String(err?.message || err);\n      console.warn('[kit] WebGPU start failed, using WebGL2:', fellBack);\n      renderer = await build(false);\n    }\n  } else {\n    renderer = await build(false);\n  }\n\n  const active = renderer.backend?.isWebGPUBackend ? 'webgpu' : 'webgl2';\n  if (wanted === 'webgpu' && active !== 'webgpu' && !fellBack) fellBack = 'three.js fell back to WebGL2';\n\n  const reportLost = renderer.onDeviceLost.bind(renderer);\n  renderer.onDeviceLost = (info) => {\n    reportLost(info);\n    if (active === 'webgpu') deviceMemory.write({ webgpuFailed: Date.now(), reason: String(info?.message || 'device lost') });\n    onDeviceLost?.(info, active);\n  };\n\n  return { renderer, backend: active, adapterInfo, fellBack };\n}\n\n/** A human label for HUDs and the settings panel. */\nexport const backendLabel = (backend) => (backend === 'webgpu' ? 'WebGPU' : 'WebGL2');","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01D4FgYrBoqtCYYi87RsmDtU","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
