{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjErqyvmJX1P4Rvw3d6Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01SzeJhjxaqtkokR3SC3wECz","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-215625__x13c81f/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":130},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8169,"cache_read_input_tokens":321739,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8169},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9db0da04-284f-40b5-8e80-2e3f7ed2ab1c","uuid":"9577acd0-941d-4b20-adcb-203e1d4b7d47","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:36:41.548Z","request_id":"req_011CgjErqgpnc8VkmV9MDfkZ","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01SzeJhjxaqtkokR3SC3wECz":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-215625__x13c81f/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":130}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SzeJhjxaqtkokR3SC3wECz","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Build plan\n2\t\n3\tYou build this game alone, from this file and TASK.md. TASK.md wins any conflict.\n4\t\n5\t- **Paths** are relative to your dev folder (the one TASK.md tells you to create). The finished single HTML goes where TASK.md says, under the name TASK.md gives.\n6\t- **The title is yours.** Pick it as TASK.md says. Nothing in the fiction depends on it. Keep it in one constant (`src/content/identity.js`) used by the title screen, the page `<title>`, About and the hub card. Below it is just \"the game\".\n7\t- **Everything here ships working.** No TODOs, placeholders or stubs. If you run short, make the game shorter, never thinner: cut encounter sizes, side rooms and decorative prefabs before any mechanic. Every feature in §8.7 must still happen in a real play of the game.\n8\t- **Work in the order of §16.** Each milestone leaves a playable game.\n9\t- **§2 was tested on this computer for this plan,** except where a line says otherwise. Trust it over your memory of Three.js and Rapier. Extra research is your choice; none is required.\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## 1. The game\n14\t\n15\t**Pitch.** A straight-faced B-movie in inked pulp-comic style. Postal Inspector Dash Mercer is sent to the remote Principality of Zlatovia to bring back the President's 74-year-old mother, who was kidnapped from a beekeeping retreat by a honey-farming valley whose people have been \"Sweetened\" by a parasitic hive. The kidnappers mailed a ransom note, which made it a postal matter.\n16\t\n17\t**Genre and camera.** Third-person, over-the-shoulder action-horror shooter with survival-horror resource management, plus on-rails, turret, stealth and quick-time set-pieces. The camera sits behind the right shoulder and tightens when aiming. Scoped weapons go first-person. Exit-interview catalogue genre: `Horror`.\n18\t\n19\t**Length.** 45–60 minutes for a first-time player: 5 chapters, 16 zones, 4 bosses, plus the Harvest Siege arena mode (solo, couch co-op, AI Hub co-op).\n20\t\n21\t**Originality rule.** TASK.md asks for an original game inspired by the source. Use its mechanics and pacing. Use nothing else from it: no names, characters, dialogue, catchphrases, music, level layouts, creature designs or visual style. Everything in the table's right column is this game's own.\n22\t\n23\t| What makes the source distinctive | This game's own form |\n24\t|---|---|\n25\t| Lone quipping agent, VIP rescue | Postal Inspector Dash Mercer; postal puns are her one-liners |\n26\t| Young VIP you escort | Mrs. Winifred \"Winnie\" Pruitt, 74, the First Mother: bridge shark, handbag, zero gratitude |\n27\t| Hostile rural cult, parasite control | The Sweet Communion of the Medovina Valley; hosts are \"the Sweetened\", ruled by a hive queen |\n28\t| Hammy villains, radio taunts | Mayor Slatko (megaphone), Prior Vosk (organ, stilts), Trent \"Dead Letter\" Vail (ex-partner) |\n29\t| Laser sight, planted feet, shoot-then-melee crowd control | Same loop, tuned in §4–5, with an ink comic hit language |\n30\t| Grid inventory case | The Parcel Case |\n31\t| Wandering weapon merchant | Mr. Fenwick, relentlessly cheerful door-to-door arms salesman |\n32\t| Save points with calm music | Rural mailboxes: \"Mail a Report\" |\n33\t| Rural crowd siege, power-tool stalker | Barricading the village Post Office; the Harvester |\n34\t| Giant, knife duel, cart ride, regenerating horrors, blind stalker, partner interlude, shooting gallery, arena mode | Wicker Effigy, letter-opener duel, runaway honey wagon, Wax Effigies with UV scope, the Penitent, playing as Winnie, Fenwick's Test Range, Harvest Siege |\n35\t\n36\t**Pillars.** Every design call serves one of these.\n37\t\n38\t1. **Stand and deliver.** Aiming plants your feet. The laser wavers, then settles. Patience is rewarded and panic is punished.\n39\t2. **One bullet, one boot.** Shoot a head and kick the crowd. Shoot a knee and slam the kneeler. Melee costs no ammo and hits groups, so good play is cheap.\n40\t3. **Greed and squeeze.** A case that's never big enough, ammo that's nearly enough, treasure worth a detour, a salesman who always has what you can't quite afford.\n41\t4. **Straight-faced camp.** Nobody in the fiction knows it's funny. Villains monologue, the hero quips, and the horror is still real.\n42\t5. **Something new every five minutes.** No zone repeats another zone's idea.\n43\t\n44\t---\n45\t\n46\t## 2. Tested facts and recipes\n47\t\n48\t### 2.1 Versions and imports\n49\t\n50\t```\n51\tnpm i three@0.180.0 @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3 playwright@1.63.0 \\\n52\t      @fontsource/bangers@5.3.0 @fontsource/barlow-condensed@5.3.0 @fontsource/special-elite@5.3.0 @fontsource/creepster@5.3.0\n53\t```\n54\t\n55\t- `esbuild` 0.28.2 is a global CLI here; call it with `execFileSync('esbuild', …)`.\n56\t- Import only from `three/webgpu`, `three/tsl` and `three/addons/…`. An addon that imports bare `three` is fine: in r180 both share `three.core.js` (checked: same `Object3D`).\n57\t- Do **not** add an esbuild alias for `three`. Aliases match by prefix and break `three/webgpu`.\n58\t- r180's class is `THREE.PostProcessing`. Newer docs call it `RenderPipeline`.\n59\t- The AI Hub repo's `lib/kit` targets r186 and Rapier 0.20. Don't use it; TASK.md's versions win.\n60\t\n61\t### 2.2 Build: one file, everything inline\n62\t\n63\tInline Three.js, Rapier, fonts and the hub client. No CDN: it's 3–4 MB, opens from `file://`, runs offline, and a session has no network questions to answer. Tested shape of `tools/build.mjs`:\n64\t\n65\t```js\n66\texecFileSync('esbuild', ['src/main.js','--bundle','--format=esm','--target=es2022','--minify',\n67\t  '--loader:.woff2=dataurl','--outfile=dist/bundle.js','--log-level=warning'], { stdio:'inherit' });\n68\tconst client = readFileSync('hub/session.js','utf8').replace(/\\n$/,'');       // never minify or edit it\n69\tconst bundle = readFileSync('dist/bundle.js','utf8');                         // esbuild already escapes </script\n70\twriteFileSync('dist/game.html', ['<!doctype html>','<html lang=\"en\"><head>','<meta charset=\"utf-8\">',\n71\t  '<script>',client,'</script>', HEAD_TAGS, `<style>${css}</style>`, '</head><body>',\n72\t  '<script type=\"module\">',bundle,'</script>','</body></html>',''].join('\\n'));   // join, never String.replace: bundles contain $&\n73\t```\n74\t\n75\t- Tests use `dist/game.html`. A `--release` flag copies it to the deliverable path from TASK.md.\n76\t- Fonts: `import url from '@fontsource/bangers/files/bangers-latin-400-normal.woff2'`, then `document.fonts.add(await new FontFace('Bangers', \\`url(${url})\\`).load())`. Same pattern for the other faces (`barlow-condensed-latin-600-normal`, `special-elite-latin-400-normal`, `creepster-latin-400-normal`). Tested from `file://`, including use in 2D canvases.\n77\t\n78\t`HEAD_TAGS`, each `<meta>` written exactly as `name` then `content` (the hub reads them with a regex):\n79\t\n80\t```html\n81\t<title>{TITLE} — {tagline}</title>\n82\t<meta name=\"description\" content=\"{one or two sentences}\">\n83\t<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#d9951f\">\n84\t<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:image/svg+xml,…a postage stamp with a bee…\">\n85\t<meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"Resident Evil 4\">\n86\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n87\t<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n88\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n89\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n90\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n91\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"6\">\n92\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Survive the Harvest Siege and top the score\">\n93\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Stick/WASD move · L2/RMB aim · R2/LMB fire · Cross/E kick · Square/R reload\">\n94\t```\n95\t\n96\tDo not add `hub:webgpu`: the game falls back to WebGL2. Goal stays under 60 characters, howto under 80.\n97\t\n98\t### 2.3 Headless browser\n99\t\n100\tLaunch Chromium with **all five** flags, on the default headless shell:\n101\t\n102\t```js\n103\tconst browser = await chromium.launch({ args: ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan',\n104\t  '--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader'] });\n105\t```\n106\t\n107\t- **TASK.md's three flags are not enough.** With only those, the device is lost right after the first render (\"A valid external Instance reference no longer exists\"), nothing draws, screenshots stay blank, and a frame counter still climbs to a false 60 fps.\n108\t- With all five, `page.screenshot()` shows the real WebGPU canvas, and a heavy scene runs at 1–3 fps.\n109\t- `channel:'chromium'` (new headless) aborts on this machine.\n110\t- A frame count proves nothing. Always watch these, and fail the test on any:\n111\t\n112\t```js\n113\trenderer.backend.device.lost.then(i => state.deviceLost = i.message);\n114\trenderer.backend.device.addEventListener('uncapturederror', e => state.gpuErrors.push(e.error.message));\n115\t```\n116\t\n117\t- Rapier's \"using deprecated parameters for the initialization function\" warning is harmless.\n118\t- Under pointer lock, Playwright's `page.mouse.move` produces absurd `movementX` values. Test mouse-look by dispatching `new MouseEvent('mousemove',{movementX,movementY})`.\n119\t- Firefox launched with default settings got no WebGL context here and threw inside `renderer.init()`. So catch that: if neither backend starts, show a plain \"needs WebGPU or WebGL 2\" card (in a session, call `AIHub.fail`). Run browser tests on Chromium; use WebKit for the fallback and phone profiles.\n120\t\n121\t### 2.4 Rendering limits that fail silently\n122\t\n123\t| Trap | What happens | Do this |\n124\t|---|---|---|\n125\t| `renderer.compileAsync()` with an MRT pass | Invalid pipelines (\"Color target has no corresponding fragment stage output\"); objects vanish | Never call it. Warm up by placing one of every prefab in view behind the loader and `await post.renderAsync()` once |\n126\t| More than 7 shadow-casting lights | Each costs 2 of the 16 sampled textures in every lit material. The 8th fails validation and materials disappear with no exception | Max **6** at once: sun plus a pool of 5. Non-shadow lights are effectively free (64 point lights on one toon material tested fine). Use them lavishly; TASK.md's unlimited budget still can't exceed this |\n127\t| Adding/removing lights, or toggling `visible`, during play | Changes the light set, which rebuilds lit materials and hitches (three's design; not timed here) | Fixed pool per zone; set `intensity = 0` |\n128\t| `computeVertexNormals()` on non-indexed geometry | Every facet becomes an ink line | Keep the primitives' smooth normals; merge indexed geometry |\n129\t| Compute shaders | WebGL2 fallback has none (three's design; not run here) | Animate FX in TSL (`instanceIndex`, `time`). 2,600 TSL bees tested on both backends |\n130\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9db0da04-284f-40b5-8e80-2e3f7ed2ab1c","uuid":"cc0e859b-70ae-435c-ab37-f45074411078","timestamp":"2026-11-05T05:36:41.562Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan-v2__20261104-215625__x13c81f/work/PLAN.md","content":"# Build plan\n\nYou build this game alone, from this file and TASK.md. TASK.md wins any conflict.\n\n- **Paths** are relative to your dev folder (the one TASK.md tells you to create). The finished single HTML goes where TASK.md says, under the name TASK.md gives.\n- **The title is yours.** Pick it as TASK.md says. Nothing in the fiction depends on it. Keep it in one constant (`src/content/identity.js`) used by the title screen, the page `<title>`, About and the hub card. Below it is just \"the game\".\n- **Everything here ships working.** No TODOs, placeholders or stubs. If you run short, make the game shorter, never thinner: cut encounter sizes, side rooms and decorative prefabs before any mechanic. Every feature in §8.7 must still happen in a real play of the game.\n- **Work in the order of §16.** Each milestone leaves a playable game.\n- **§2 was tested on this computer for this plan,** except where a line says otherwise. Trust it over your memory of Three.js and Rapier. Extra research is your choice; none is required.\n\n---\n\n## 1. The game\n\n**Pitch.** A straight-faced B-movie in inked pulp-comic style. Postal Inspector Dash Mercer is sent to the remote Principality of Zlatovia to bring back the President's 74-year-old mother, who was kidnapped from a beekeeping retreat by a honey-farming valley whose people have been \"Sweetened\" by a parasitic hive. The kidnappers mailed a ransom note, which made it a postal matter.\n\n**Genre and camera.** Third-person, over-the-shoulder action-horror shooter with survival-horror resource management, plus on-rails, turret, stealth and quick-time set-pieces. The camera sits behind the right shoulder and tightens when aiming. Scoped weapons go first-person. Exit-interview catalogue genre: `Horror`.\n\n**Length.** 45–60 minutes for a first-time player: 5 chapters, 16 zones, 4 bosses, plus the Harvest Siege arena mode (solo, couch co-op, AI Hub co-op).\n\n**Originality rule.** TASK.md asks for an original game inspired by the source. Use its mechanics and pacing. Use nothing else from it: no names, characters, dialogue, catchphrases, music, level layouts, creature designs or visual style. Everything in the table's right column is this game's own.\n\n| What makes the source distinctive | This game's own form |\n|---|---|\n| Lone quipping agent, VIP rescue | Postal Inspector Dash Mercer; postal puns are her one-liners |\n| Young VIP you escort | Mrs. Winifred \"Winnie\" Pruitt, 74, the First Mother: bridge shark, handbag, zero gratitude |\n| Hostile rural cult, parasite control | The Sweet Communion of the Medovina Valley; hosts are \"the Sweetened\", ruled by a hive queen |\n| Hammy villains, radio taunts | Mayor Slatko (megaphone), Prior Vosk (organ, stilts), Trent \"Dead Letter\" Vail (ex-partner) |\n| Laser sight, planted feet, shoot-then-melee crowd control | Same loop, tuned in §4–5, with an ink comic hit language |\n| Grid inventory case | The Parcel Case |\n| Wandering weapon merchant | Mr. Fenwick, relentlessly cheerful door-to-door arms salesman |\n| Save points with calm music | Rural mailboxes: \"Mail a Report\" |\n| Rural crowd siege, power-tool stalker | Barricading the village Post Office; the Harvester |\n| Giant, knife duel, cart ride, regenerating horrors, blind stalker, partner interlude, shooting gallery, arena mode | Wicker Effigy, letter-opener duel, runaway honey wagon, Wax Effigies with UV scope, the Penitent, playing as Winnie, Fenwick's Test Range, Harvest Siege |\n\n**Pillars.** Every design call serves one of these.\n\n1. **Stand and deliver.** Aiming plants your feet. The laser wavers, then settles. Patience is rewarded and panic is punished.\n2. **One bullet, one boot.** Shoot a head and kick the crowd. Shoot a knee and slam the kneeler. Melee costs no ammo and hits groups, so good play is cheap.\n3. **Greed and squeeze.** A case that's never big enough, ammo that's nearly enough, treasure worth a detour, a salesman who always has what you can't quite afford.\n4. **Straight-faced camp.** Nobody in the fiction knows it's funny. Villains monologue, the hero quips, and the horror is still real.\n5. **Something new every five minutes.** No zone repeats another zone's idea.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tested facts and recipes\n\n### 2.1 Versions and imports\n\n```\nnpm i three@0.180.0 @dimforge/rapier3d-compat@0.19.3 playwright@1.63.0 \\\n      @fontsource/bangers@5.3.0 @fontsource/barlow-condensed@5.3.0 @fontsource/special-elite@5.3.0 @fontsource/creepster@5.3.0\n```\n\n- `esbuild` 0.28.2 is a global CLI here; call it with `execFileSync('esbuild', …)`.\n- Import only from `three/webgpu`, `three/tsl` and `three/addons/…`. An addon that imports bare `three` is fine: in r180 both share `three.core.js` (checked: same `Object3D`).\n- Do **not** add an esbuild alias for `three`. Aliases match by prefix and break `three/webgpu`.\n- r180's class is `THREE.PostProcessing`. Newer docs call it `RenderPipeline`.\n- The AI Hub repo's `lib/kit` targets r186 and Rapier 0.20. Don't use it; TASK.md's versions win.\n\n### 2.2 Build: one file, everything inline\n\nInline Three.js, Rapier, fonts and the hub client. No CDN: it's 3–4 MB, opens from `file://`, runs offline, and a session has no network questions to answer. Tested shape of `tools/build.mjs`:\n\n```js\nexecFileSync('esbuild', ['src/main.js','--bundle','--format=esm','--target=es2022','--minify',\n  '--loader:.woff2=dataurl','--outfile=dist/bundle.js','--log-level=warning'], { stdio:'inherit' });\nconst client = readFileSync('hub/session.js','utf8').replace(/\\n$/,'');       // never minify or edit it\nconst bundle = readFileSync('dist/bundle.js','utf8');                         // esbuild already escapes </script\nwriteFileSync('dist/game.html', ['<!doctype html>','<html lang=\"en\"><head>','<meta charset=\"utf-8\">',\n  '<script>',client,'</script>', HEAD_TAGS, `<style>${css}</style>`, '</head><body>',\n  '<script type=\"module\">',bundle,'</script>','</body></html>',''].join('\\n'));   // join, never String.replace: bundles contain $&\n```\n\n- Tests use `dist/game.html`. A `--release` flag copies it to the deliverable path from TASK.md.\n- Fonts: `import url from '@fontsource/bangers/files/bangers-latin-400-normal.woff2'`, then `document.fonts.add(await new FontFace('Bangers', \\`url(${url})\\`).load())`. Same pattern for the other faces (`barlow-condensed-latin-600-normal`, `special-elite-latin-400-normal`, `creepster-latin-400-normal`). Tested from `file://`, including use in 2D canvases.\n\n`HEAD_TAGS`, each `<meta>` written exactly as `name` then `content` (the hub reads them with a regex):\n\n```html\n<title>{TITLE} — {tagline}</title>\n<meta name=\"description\" content=\"{one or two sentences}\">\n<meta name=\"theme-color\" content=\"#d9951f\">\n<link rel=\"icon\" href=\"data:image/svg+xml,…a postage stamp with a bee…\">\n<meta name=\"hub:source\" content=\"Resident Evil 4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1–4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"1-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"6\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Survive the Harvest Siege and top the score\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Stick/WASD move · L2/RMB aim · R2/LMB fire · Cross/E kick · Square/R reload\">\n```\n\nDo not add `hub:webgpu`: the game falls back to WebGL2. Goal stays under 60 characters, howto under 80.\n\n### 2.3 Headless browser\n\nLaunch Chromium with **all five** flags, on the default headless shell:\n\n```js\nconst browser = await chromium.launch({ args: ['--enable-unsafe-webgpu','--enable-features=Vulkan',\n  '--use-vulkan=swiftshader','--use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader','--use-angle=swiftshader'] });\n```\n\n- **TASK.md's three flags are not enough.** With only those, the device is lost right after the first render (\"A valid external Instance reference no longer exists\"), nothing draws, screenshots stay blank, and a frame counter still climbs to a false 60 fps.\n- With all five, `page.screenshot()` shows the real WebGPU canvas, and a heavy scene runs at 1–3 fps.\n- `channel:'chromium'` (new headless) aborts on this machine.\n- A frame count proves nothing. Always watch these, and fail the test on any:\n\n```js\nrenderer.backend.device.lost.then(i => state.deviceLost = i.message);\nrenderer.backend.device.addEventListener('uncapturederror', e => state.gpuErrors.push(e.error.message));\n```\n\n- Rapier's \"using deprecated parameters for the initialization function\" warning is harmless.\n- Under pointer lock, Playwright's `page.mouse.move` produces absurd `movementX` values. Test mouse-look by dispatching `new MouseEvent('mousemove',{movementX,movementY})`.\n- Firefox launched with default settings got no WebGL context here and threw inside `renderer.init()`. So catch that: if neither backend starts, show a plain \"needs WebGPU or WebGL 2\" card (in a session, call `AIHub.fail`). Run browser tests on Chromium; use WebKit for the fallback and phone profiles.\n\n### 2.4 Rendering limits that fail silently\n\n| Trap | What happens | Do this |\n|---|---|---|\n| `renderer.compileAsync()` with an MRT pass | Invalid pipelines (\"Color target has no corresponding fragment stage output\"); objects vanish | Never call it. Warm up by placing one of every prefab in view behind the loader and `await post.renderAsync()` once |\n| More than 7 shadow-casting lights | Each costs 2 of the 16 sampled textures in every lit material. The 8th fails validation and materials disappear with no exception | Max **6** at once: sun plus a pool of 5. Non-shadow lights are effectively free (64 point lights on one toon material tested fine). Use them lavishly; TASK.md's unlimited budget still can't exceed this |\n| Adding/removing lights, or toggling `visible`, during play | Changes the light set, which rebuilds lit materials and hitches (three's design; not timed here) | Fixed pool per zone; set `intensity = 0` |\n| `computeVertexNormals()` on non-indexed geometry | Every facet becomes an ink line | Keep the primitives' smooth normals; merge indexed geometry |\n| Compute shaders | WebGL2 fallback has none (three's design; not run here) | Animate FX in TSL (`instanceIndex`, `time`). 2,600 TSL bees tested on both backends |\n","numLines":130,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1149}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SzeJhjxaqtkokR3SC3wECz","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
